{"parentUuid":"30244f35-1694-4aa3-af1a-87019b7cb055","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"11e6e8df-3ad8-4680-bc8e-6b9f44e96139","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"<command-message>backup</command-message>\n<command-name>/backup</command-name>"},"uuid":"f5c3ffd4-e6b2-4dc2-a4ca-06e46f427581","timestamp":"2026-10-07T16:39:14.230Z","origin":{"kind":"human"},"turnOrigin":"human","turnPosition":{"promptIndex":1,"turnIndex":1},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"c8cd7806-9ebb-4a31-9ea2-52a66b3eb4e0","version":"2.1.292","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"f5c3ffd4-e6b2-4dc2-a4ca-06e46f427581","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"11e6e8df-3ad8-4680-bc8e-6b9f44e96139","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Base directory for this skill: /Users/kristinarash/.claude/skills/backup\n\n# Backup\n\nRuns `~/.claude/scripts/daily-backup.ps1` right now. Same script the `com.keystone.dailybackup`\nlaunchd agent runs at 21:00 daily (moved from 09:00 on 2026-09-24, when the Mac became the evening machine) — this is the on-demand path for when the Mac was shut at 21:00.\n\n## Run it\n\nDo not pre-check anything. Run first, report after.\n\n```bash\n/usr/bin/caffeinate -i /opt/homebrew/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -File ~/.claude/scripts/daily-backup.ps1 2>&1 | tail -40\n```\n\nUse a Bash `timeout` of `600000` (10 min). An awake run takes ~5-10 min, most of it the memsearch\nINDEX step. Keep `caffeinate -i`: without it the Mac idle-sleeps on battery the moment the copies\nfinish, and INDEX crawls through brief dark wakes. On 2026-10-06 that turned a ~6 min index into\n2 h. If a run outlasts the timeout, it moves to the background; wait for it, do not re-run.\n\n## Read the verdict from state, not from the tail\n\n```bash\ncat ~/.claude/scripts/logs/backup-state.json\n```\n\n`status` is `OK` or `ERROR`; `errors` lists the failed job codes. That file is the authority.\nThe tail is for diagnosing, not for deciding.\n\n**`status: OK` is not \"everything was saved\" — read `okCount` too.** A run that skipped the\nSSD still ends `OK` with 0 errors. See the SSD gotcha below for what the count means.\n\n## Then clear the stale hook error, if any\n\nA failed run appends a line to `~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log`, which is surfaced\nas a HOOK FAILURE at every session start. A later successful run does **not** clear it. If `status`\nis now `OK` and the file contains only `daily-backup:` lines, archive and empty it:\n\n```bash\ncp ~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log ~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log.archived-$(date +%Y-%m-%d)\ncp /dev/null ~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log\n```\n\nIf any non-`daily-backup` line is present, leave the file alone and tell the user what else is in it.\nShell truncation (`: > file`) under `$HOME` is blocked by dcg rule\n`core.filesystem:redirect-truncate-root-home`, and there is no `truncate` binary here — use `cp /dev/null`.\n\n## Report\n\nThree lines, plain: did it work, what was skipped, what to do next. Never report \"backup complete\"\nwithout naming a skipped target — a silent partial backup is the failure this skill exists to prevent.\n\n## Gotchas\n\n**Safe to ignore — expected on a healthy run:**\n- `SNAP skip (missing): ~/.codex/memories` — that path has never existed.\n- `TARGET baylor deprecated in manifest - snapshots to it SKIPPED` — deliberate.\n\n**Real gaps — always surface these to the user:**\n- `TARGET SSD /Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup NOT MOUNTED - orphan and working-tree capture SKIPPED`\n  — the run still reports `OK` with 0 errors, but **untracked, gitignored, and uncommitted-modified\n  files were not saved anywhere.** GitHub and Google Drive cover only committed and pushed work.\n  Tell the user to plug in CRASHBOX and run again. This is the most likely way to lose real work.\n\n  **Cross-check with `okCount`, because the error count cannot see this.** `okCount` in\n  `backup-state.json` is the number of jobs that ran, and two whole job classes — `CAPTURE`\n  (per-project working-tree files) and `STATE-SSD` — exist only when the SSD is mounted. So:\n  - **~31 jobs** = SSD captured. **~9-10 jobs** = SSD was absent; only the 4 `REPO` pushes and\n    6 Drive `SNAP`s ran (verified against runs on 2026-09-04 and 2026-09-05).\n  - A single-digit `okCount` with `status: OK` is the silent partial backup. Say so out loud.\n  - Do not derive this from the error count or the exit code — both read `OK` either way.\n- `TARGET ... NOT MOUNTED - is Google Drive for Desktop running?` — the Drive app is not running.\n  Start it, then re-run.\n- `PUSH ERR ... Could not resolve host: github.com` — a network blip, not a repo problem. Confirm\n  with `ping -c1 github.com`, then just re-run; the commits are already made locally and will push.\n  (Seen 2026-09-04 09:05: both `global-memory` and `skills` failed this way and a re-run fixed both.)\n- `repo-diverged:<name>` — automation will never merge. Reconcile that repo by hand. For\n  `dotclaude` and `global-memory` this almost always means the other machine pushed; `/dev-sync`\n  Step 1 merges and pushes both (seen 2026-09-15).\n- `STATE-SSD .codex : FAILED` with `rsync: error: skills/<name>: unlinkat: Directory not empty`\n  just above it in `daily-backup.log` (the error only reaches the log file, not stdout). Cause:\n  `sync-to-codex.sh` turned `~/.codex/skills/<name>` into a symlink, but the SSD copy is still a\n  real folder. The copy is additive (no `--delete`, no `--force`), so rsync cannot swap a\n  non-empty folder for a link — and it reports only some names per run, so fixing one reveals the\n  next. List them all at once:\n  `cd ~/.codex/skills && for e in *; do [ -L \"$e\" ] && [ -d \"/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup/codex/skills/$e\" ] && [ ! -L \"/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup/codex/skills/$e\" ] && echo \"$e\"; done`\n  Move each one (literal paths — dcg blocks `mv` with `$VAR` paths) into\n  `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup/_OLD_codex-skills-dirs-<date>/`, then re-run. Never `rm`:\n  the skills also live in the `claude-skills` repo, but the move is free. (Seen 2026-09-15:\n  `dev-sync`, `new-project`, `startup`, `closeout`.)\n\n**Other:**\n- A nightly INDEX that takes hours (`INDEX path set` to `INDEX full set` in `daily-backup.log`) is the\n  Mac asleep, not memsearch. With the lid closed at 21:00, launchd's `caffeinate -i -s` cannot keep it\n  awake, so INDEX runs only in short dark wakes (`pmset -g log | grep Sleep` shows it). It still finishes.\n- Every log line appears **twice** for launchd runs and once for manual runs. The script both writes\n  to the log file and prints to stdout, and the plist captures stdout to the same file. Not a bug —\n  do not \"fix\" it, and do not read doubled lines as two attempts.\n- The script is PowerShell only because `pwsh` runs it natively. It manages launchd, not Task\n  Scheduler; the Windows original is parked at `daily-backup.ps1.win-orig`.\n- `-DryRun` is the only parameter. Use it to preview without writing.\n\n## Examples\n\n**Example 1: routine catch-up**\n```\nUser: \"run the backup\"\n→ Runs the script, reads backup-state.json (status OK, 0 errors)\n→ \"Backup done, no errors. Everything pushed and copied to Drive.\n   CRASHBOX SSD wasn't plugged in, so uncommitted files were not saved.\n   Plug it in and run /backup again if you have unsaved work.\"\n```\n\n**Example 2: session start shows a hook failure**\n```\nUser: \"the backup failed this morning — fix it\"\n→ Greps daily-backup.log for the error codes → DNS failure on both pushes\n→ Confirms github.com reachable, re-runs the script → status OK\n→ Archives and empties .errors.log so the warning stops appearing\n```\n"}]},"isMeta":true,"turnCompanion":true,"uuid":"e328a0d3-68ab-4d03-b915-8c964b852ec9","timestamp":"2026-10-07T16:39:14.230Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"c8cd7806-9ebb-4a31-9ea2-52a66b3eb4e0","version":"2.1.292","gitBranch":"main"}
